      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThe markdown is GLM-OCR output but it appears to be a ChatGPT-style summary of pages 2 and 3 of an OIML Certificate, not the raw OCR. Regardless, I need to extract what's present.\n\nPage 2 content:\n- OIML Certificate No.: R76/2006-A-DK2-2023.07\n- Issuing Authority: FORCE Certification A/S\n- Date of Issue: 24 October 2023\n- Signed by: Jens Hovgård Jensen, Certification Manager\n\nReports:\n- Type examination report: 122-29322.10, 30 March 2023, 119 pages\n- Type evaluation report: 122-29322.90.20, 04 September 2023, 27 pages\n- Documentation file no. 122-29322\n\nCertificate History:\n- Initial version, 24 October 2023\n\nPage 3 content (Descriptive Annex):\nCharacteristics:\n- Type: ADW-E / ADW-C / AXM / AGS / VGS\n- Accuracy Class: III and IIII\n- Weighing ranges: Single-interval, multi-interval or multi-range\n- Maximum capacity (Max): 0.3 kg to 30 kg\n- Minimum capacity (Min): 20 × e\n- Max verification scale intervals (n): ≤6000 (single interval); ≤2×5000 (multi-interval/multi-range)\n- Verification scale interval (e): ≥0.05 g\n- Maximum tare effect: −Max\n- Operating temperature range: −10 °C / +40 °C\n- Power supply: 12VDC external (100–240VAC 50/60 Hz) or 6VDC rechargeable battery\n\nSoftware Identification:\n- AXM & ADW-C → CE033\n- ADW-E → CE031 and CE032\n- AGS & VGS → CE034\n\nPermitted Devices, Interfaces...\n\nNow let me build the JSON according to the layered schema.\n\n**certificate:**\n- number: \"R76/2006-A-DK2-2023.07\" — hmm, canonical form. OIML R76 scheme. The number as given: R76/2006-A-DK2-2023.07. I'll use that.\n- scheme: \"A\" (it's R76/2006-A-..., scheme A)\n- project_number: not clearly present. Could be 122-29322 is the project number (documentation file). Not stated as project number; leave out or maybe not.\n- page_total: 3? The fragment says \"page 2 of 3\". So total pages 3. Include page_total: 3.\n- member_state: \"DK\" (from DK2 in cert number; Denmark). The cert number includes DK. I could infer member_state: \"Denmark\" — hmm, rule 8: don't fabricate. But DK is present in cert number. The issuing authority FORCE Certification A/S is Danish. The page says \"issuing Member State\" mentioned but not named. I think member_state: \"DK\" is reasonably supported by the certificate number. I'll include \"DK\".\n- date_issued: \"2023-10-24\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present. Omit.\n\n**issuing_authority:**\n- name: \"FORCE Certification A/S\"\n- person_responsible: \"Jens Hovgård Jensen\"\n- person_title: \"Certification Manager\"\n- address_lines: not present, omit.\n- oiml_issuer_id: omit.\n\n**applicants[] / manufacturers[]:** Not present in this fragment. Omit.\n\n**certified_type:**\n- category: \"Indicator\" — these types (ADW-E, ADW-C, AXM, AGS, VGS) are weighing indicators? Actually R76 covers non-automatic weighing instruments. ADW could be \"ADW\" indicator from A&D. AXM, AGS, VGS... These are likely indicators/weighing instruments. Hmm. The certificate is R76 (non-automatic weighing instruments). The category could be \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\". Given \"Weighing ranges\", \"Maximum capacity 0.3 kg to 30 kg\", it's a weighing instrument or indicator. I'd say category \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\". But to be safe: R76 = Non-automatic weighing instruments. The types are ADW-E / ADW-C / AXM / AGS / VGS. A&D Instruments: ADW is a weighing module; AXM... Actually these look like A&D weighing indicators. Category: \"Indicator\"? Hmm. Without more context, I'll use \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" since it's under R76. Actually, maybe safest: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\".\n\n- type_designations: [\"ADW-E\", \"ADW-C\", \"AXM\", \"AGS\", \"VGS\"]\n- module_designation: null? Not mentioned. Omit.\n- description: not really present. Omit.\n\n**model_family:**\nThe cert covers 5 type designations: ADW-E, ADW-C, AXM, AGS, VGS. These are model variants. Software ID varies by model:\n- AXM & ADW-C → CE033\n- ADW-E → CE031 and CE032\n- AGS & VGS → CE034\n\nSo model_family.models:\n- {\"model_id\": \"ADW-E\", \"attributes\": {\"software_identification\": \"CE031, CE032\"}}\n- {\"model_id\": \"ADW-C\", \"attributes\": {\"software_identification\": \"CE033\"}}\n- {\"model_id\": \"AXM\", \"attributes\": {\"software_identification\": \"CE033\"}}\n- {\"model_id\": \"AGS\", \"attributes\": {\"software_identification\": \"CE034\"}}\n- {\"model_id\": \"VGS\", \"attributes\": {\"software_identification\": \"CE034\"}}\n\nfamily_name: null.\n\n**characteristics:**\n\ntype_level:\n- accuracy_class: \"III and IIII\" — how to represent? Could be value as string [\"III\", \"IIII\"] — the schema wants value as number|string. Use \"III, IIII\" string or array? The shape says value: <number | {min,max} | string>. So string \"III, IIII\". Hmm, accuracy_classes in recommendation has accuracy_classes[]. For characteristics, use value: \"III, IIII\" or maybe list not allowed. I'll use string.\n\nActually maybe I could skip type_level accuracy class since recommendation.accuracy_classes captures it. But including is fine. Let me include:\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"III, IIII\", unit_symbol: null}\n\nHmm, or as value: [\"III\", \"IIII\"]? Strictly schema says number|string. I'll use string \"III and IIII\" → better \"III, IIII\".\n\n- weighing_ranges: {value: \"Single-interval, multi-interval or multi-range\"} — string value.\n- maximum_capacity: {value: {min: 0.3, max: 30}, unit_symbol: \"kg\"}\n- minimum_capacity: {value: \"20 × e\"} — formula, string. Hmm \"20 × e\" contains ×; that's fine, it's not LaTeX. Keep as \"20 × e\" or \"20e\". I'll use \"20 × e\". Actually canonical: value: \"20 × e\", unit_symbol: null. Or \"20e\". I'll use \"20 × e\".\n\nWait — rule 1 says never include LaTeX or footnote markers. \"20 × e\" is fine.\n\n- max_verification_scale_intervals: For single interval ≤6000; multi-interval/multi-range ≤2×5000. This is a config_level item (varies by configuration: single-interval vs multi-interval/multi-range). Or type_level with two conditions. Better as config_level with axis \"weighing_range_type\".\n\nconfig_level:\n- attribute: \"max_verification_scale_intervals\", axis: \"weighing_range_type\", values: [{condition: \"single interval\", value: {max: 6000, min: null}, unit_symbol: null}, {condition: \"multi-interval or multi-range\", value: ...}]\n\nHmm, \"≤2×5000\" — the max is 2 × 5000 = 10000. The value \"2×5000\" could be formula string \"2×5000\" or numeric 10000. I'd keep \"2×5000\"? Value rules: formulas keep as string. \"≤2×5000\" → {min: null, max: \"2×5000\"}? That's awkward since max should be a number. Alternatively represent value as string \"≤2×5000\" with min/max null? Hmm.\n\nThe structured form: value can be {min, max} or string. For \"≤2×5000\", I could use value: {min: null, max: 10000} — computing 2×5000=10000 is not fabrication, it's arithmetic. But safer to keep as string \"2 × 5000\"? The rule says ranges become {min,max}. Open-ended: ≤6000 → {min: null, max: 6000}. For ≤2×5000, maybe value: {min: null, max: 10000} with the note. Hmm, but the per-interval constraint is 5000 and total 2×5000. I'll use {min: null, max: 10000}? Actually I think representing as string \"2×5000\" loses the structured numeric. Let me do value: {min: null, max: 10000} for the config value, since 2×5000 = 10000 total. Hmm, but maybe simpler to keep as string \"≤2×5000\". Let me think about what's most useful: downstream consumers parse numbers. 10000 is the computed bound. I'll go with 10000 and mention in the condition the formula? No, don't fabricate. The condition is \"multi-interval or multi-range\". The value ≤2×5000. I could put value: {min: null, max: 10000}. That is a faithful computation. Alternatively keep \"2×5000\" string. Rules say formulas keep as string — \"Emax/14000\" example. \"2×5000\" is a formula. So value: {min: null, max: \"2×5000\"}? Mixing types in min/max is not in the schema (value: number|string; min/max presumably numbers). To stay safe, I'll use value: {min: null, max: 10000} — arithmetic evaluation is not fabrication. Hmm, actually, I worry about \"don't fabricate\". Computing 2×5000=10000 is deterministic. I'll go with that... \n\nActually wait, let me reconsider: ≤2×5000 means the maximum number of verification scale intervals is 2 times 5000, i.e., 10000 total across two ranges, each ≤5000. Representing as max 10000 is a reasonable interpretation. But an alternative: keep as string. I think the cleanest that follows the rules: formulas keep as string. I'll represent value: {min: null, max: 10000} — no wait.\n\nLet me just decide: I'll use {\"min\": null, \"max\": 10000} for \"single interval\" condition ≤6000 → {\"min\": null, \"max\": 6000}. For multi: I'll use value: {min: null, max: 10000} with a note? No notes field. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: treat as two characteristics:\n- max_verification_scale_intervals_single_interval: {min: null, max: 6000}\n- max_verification_scale_intervals_multi: ... \n\nThe config_level approach is cleaner. I'll go with config_level and value: {min: null, max: 10000}. Actually, hold on — I think preserving \"2×5000\" matters for fidelity. The schema example shows value can be string. min/max mixing string breaks the {min,max} shape. I could put the whole thing as a string value: value: \"≤2×5000\". But rule says never include trailing punctuation and no footnote markers — \"≤2×5000\" has no forbidden chars. But structured values strict says ranges become {min,max}. Ugh.\n\nDecision: config_level value: {min: null, max: 10000}, and I'll also add unit_symbol: null. This is numeric and parseable. 2×5000=10000. Good.\n\n- verification_scale_interval: \"≥0.05 g\" → value: {min: 0.05, max: null}, unit_symbol: \"g\". This is type_level.\n- maximum_tare_effect: \"−Max\" → value: \"Max\"? Negative of Max. Represent as string \"-Max\" or \"-Emax\"? The cert says \"−Max\". Value: \"-Max\", unit_symbol: null. Hmm, \"−\" is a unicode minus; keep as \"-Max\". I'll use value: \"-Max\".\n- operating_temperature_range: \"-10 °C / +40 °C\" → value: {min: -10, max: 40}, unit_symbol: \"°C\".\n\nPower supply: \"12VDC external (100–240VAC 50/60 Hz) or 6VDC rechargeable battery\". Per rule 3, split:\n- power_supply_voltage_external: {min: 12, max: 12}? Actually 12 V DC external. value: 12, unit_symbol: \"V\", plus power_supply_type \"DC external\". Hmm.\n\nLet me structure:\n- power_supply: complex. Better to split into multiple type_level characteristics:\n  - power_supply_voltage_dc: value: 12, unit_symbol: \"V\" (external 12 VDC) — but also 6 VDC battery. Two options. Hmm.\n  - mains_voltage: {min: 100, max: 240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"\n  - mains_frequency: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"\n\nOptions: \"12VDC external (100–240VAC 50/60 Hz) or 6VDC rechargeable battery\". This is an OR of two power configurations:\nOption A: external 12 VDC adapter (which accepts 100–240 VAC 50/60 Hz mains)\nOption B: 6 VDC rechargeable battery\n\nI could do config_level with axis \"power_supply\":\n- attribute: \"power_supply_voltage\", axis: \"power_source\", values: [{condition: \"external DC supply\", value: 12, unit_symbol: \"V\"}, {condition: \"rechargeable battery\", value: 6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}]\n- type_level: mains_input_voltage {min:100,max:240} V, mains_frequency {min:50,max:60} Hz, mains_type \"AC\".\n\nAnd type_level power_supply_type: \"DC\"? Hmm. Let me do:\n\ntype_level:\n- power_supply_type: {value: \"DC\"} — covers both options (12 VDC external, 6 VDC battery).\n- mains_supply_voltage: {value: {min: 100, max: 240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- mains_supply_frequency: {value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}\n\nconfig_level:\n- attribute: \"power_supply_voltage\", axis: \"power_source\", values:\n  - {condition: \"external DC power supply\", value: 12, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n  - {condition: \"rechargeable battery\", value: 6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n\n- initial_zero_setting_range: \"≤20% Max\" → value: {min: null, max: 20}, unit_symbol: \"%\", and reference? Could add separate \"initial_zero_setting_reference\": \"Max\". Per rule 3's example (\"150% of Emax\" → safe_overload_reference), I could add reference characteristics. These are permitted devices, not exactly characteristics... The \"Permitted Devices\" section: initial zero setting ≤20% Max, semi-automatic zero setting ≤4% Max, zero tracking ≤4% Max. These are device capabilities. I could record them as type_level characteristics:\n- initial_zero_setting_range: {min: null, max: 20}, unit: \"%\", plus maybe omit reference or include reference_value. Schema for characteristics value doesn't have a reference field. I'll add separate characteristics:\n  - initial_zero_setting_range: {min: null, max: 20}, unit_symbol: \"%\"\n  - semi_automatic_zero_setting_range: {min: null, max: 4}, unit_symbol: \"%\"\n  - zero_tracking_range: {min: null, max: 4}, unit_symbol: \"%\"\nAll relative to Max. Could include \"reference\" via a sibling? Not in schema. I'll add \"zero_setting_reference\" characteristics? That might be noisy. Rule 3 says \"(Or omit if too noisy.)\" I'll include references as separate small characteristics to be faithful:\n\nHmm, the reference is the same \"Max\" for all. I could add one type_level characteristic: zero_device_range_reference: {value: \"Max\"}. Eh. I'll add a single note... Actually footnotes[] only for footnote markers. I think I'll include the three ranges and one characteristic \"percent_reference\" — no, too hacky. Simpler: include the ranges with unit \"%\" and skip the reference. Actually, let me add them as e.g. \"initial_zero_setting_range_max\" — no.\n\nAlternative: name them \"initial_zero_setting_range_percent_of_max\" etc. That encodes the reference in the attribute name. Canonical labels: `initial_zero_setting_range_percent_of_max`? Hmm, that's descriptive. Or keep simple names. I'll keep simple names: initial_zero_setting_range, semi_automatic_zero_setting_range, zero_tracking_range, with unit \"%\" — and add \"initial_zero_setting_max_reference\" — nah.\n\nActually, maybe include interface: RS232 — \"the interface does not have to be secured\". Record as type_level: interface: {value: \"RS232\"}? And permitted devices list. I might include:\n- interface_rs232_secured: {value: false}? That's interpretive. Better: interface: {value: \"RS232\"} and interface_securing_required: {value: false}? Hmm \"does not have to be secured\" → secured: no. I'll do:\n- interface: {value: \"RS232\"}\n- interface_securing_required: {value: false}\n\nMaybe that's over-interpretation but reasonable. Or combine: \"interface_rs232\": {value: \"not required to be secured\"}? I'll keep the two-field approach... Actually simpler: interface_type: \"RS232\", interface_securing: \"not required\". Let me do that as strings to avoid interpreting.\n\nPermitted devices list (printing, totalization, unit toggling g and kg only; sealed gravity compensation; tare devices; stable equilibrium/zero/net indicators). These are descriptive. Could include as type_level characteristic \"permitted_devices\": string list? Schema value is number|{min,max}|string. A long string is ugly. Maybe include as a components entry? No. I'll include:\n- permitted_zero_setting_devices... hmm.\n\nI'll include a type_level characteristic \"permitted_weighing_units\": {value: \"g, kg\"}. And maybe skip the rest, or include \"permitted_devices\" as a description string. The schema allows description only in certified_type. I could put a brief summary in certified_type.description? It's a \"Permitted Devices\" list, not a description of the type. Eh. I'll add a matrix? No.\n\nI think it's acceptable to include:\n- permitted_units: {value: \"g and kg\"}\n- tare_device_types: {value: \"semi-automatic and automatic subtractive tare devices\"}\n- gravity_compensation_device: {value: \"sealed\"}\n\nHmm, this is getting noisy. Let me be judicious: The most valuable data: characteristics, models, software IDs, reports, revisions, footnotes. I'll include zero-setting ranges (numeric), weighing units (g, kg), interface RS232, and skip the more descriptive permitted devices or fold into a \"permitted_devices\" string. I'll include a \"permitted_devices\" type_level with a semicolon-separated string? That seems acceptable and faithful. Value string: \"initial zero setting; semi-automatic zero setting; zero tracking; semi-automatic and automatic subtractive tare; printing, totalization and unit toggling (g and kg); sealed gravity compensation; stable equilibrium, zero and net indicators\". Hmm long. I'll do it — no, rule 8 is about omission; long strings are fine but noisy. I'll include it — it's real data. Actually, I'll include it as permitted_devices string. OK.\n\nmodel_level: Is there an attribute varying per model? Software ID varies. Software ID isn't numeric — I'll put it in model_family.models[].attributes.software_identification rather than characteristics.model_level (which is for attribute×model matrices). The schema example shows model_family models with just model_id, but attributes{} is in the schema: \"models[]: model_id, label, attributes{}\". Good — put software ID there.\n\n**recommendation:**\n- id: \"R76\" (canonical from \"R76/2006\")\n- edition: 2006\n- amendment: null (not present)\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [\"III\", \"IIII\"]\n\n**test_reports[]:**\n- {id: \"122-29322.10\", date: \"2023-03-30\", pages: 119, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"} — it's called \"Type examination report\". Role options: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. \"Type examination report\" → closest \"type_evaluation_report\". Yes.\n- {id: \"122-29322.90.20\", date: \"2023-09-04\", pages: 27, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"} — called \"Type evaluation report\".\n- Documentation file no. 122-29322 → test_reports entry with role \"documentation_file\", id \"122-29322\", no date/pages? pages unknown, omit pages. The schema says id, date, pages, role — omit missing.\n\n**revision_history[]:**\n- {revision: \"Initial version\", date: \"2023-10-24\", changes: \"Initial version\"}? Hmm. The table: Revision No. = \"Initial version\", Date = 24 October 2023, Description = \"–\". So revision: \"Initial version\"? The revision field is a string like \"0\". Here it literally says \"Initial version\". I'll use revision: \"0\"? No — don't fabricate; the table says \"Initial version\". I'll put revision: \"Initial version\", date: \"2023-10-24\", changes: null or omit changes (description is \"–\" meaning none). I'll omit changes.\n\n**footnotes[]:** none present.\n\n**certificate.page_total:** \"page 2 of 3\" → 3. Include.\n\nproject_number: The report numbers 122-29322... not stated as project number. Omit. Actually documentation file no. 122-29322 is a documentation file; could be project number but not labeled. Omit.\n\nmember_state: \"DK\"? The fragment mentions \"issuing Member State\" but doesn't name it explicitly... The certificate number contains \"DK\". FORCE Certification A/S is Danish (well-known). I'll include member_state: \"DK\". Hmm, risk of fabrication is low given DK in the cert number. Include.\n\ndate_issued: \"2023-10-24\".\n\nNow the model_family and software identification:\n\nmodels:\n1. ADW-E — software: CE031, CE032\n2. ADW-C — software: CE033\n3. AXM — software: CE033\n4. AGS — software: CE034\n5. VGS — software: CE034\n\nattributes: {\"software_identification\": \"CE031, CE032\"} — or array? attributes{} values can be anything I guess; I'll use string or array of strings. I'll use arrays: [\"CE031\", \"CE032\"].\n\nShould software identification also appear in characteristics.model_level? Software ID per model — model_level entries with string values would work: {attribute: \"software_identification\", values: [{model: \"ADW-E\", value: \"CE031\"}, ...]} but ADW-E has two IDs. Since model_family.models[].attributes captures it, I'll keep it there and skip model_level to avoid duplication. Hmm, but \"matrix detection — MOST IMPORTANT\" says varying attributes go in model_level. Software ID is a varying attribute. I could do both. To keep single source of truth, put in model_family.attributes. Actually let me also add model_level for software_identification since the schema emphasizes model_level... I'll put it in model_family.models[].attributes only — cleaner, schema explicitly supports it.\n\nAlso weighing ranges config: \"Single-interval, multi-interval or multi-range\" is a type-level attribute describing possible configurations. And max n varies by configuration → config_level as decided.\n\nAlso accuracy class III and IIII: class IIII is special (≥ some). Include in recommendation.accuracy_classes: [\"III\", \"IIII\"].\n\nLet me also handle \"Maximum capacity (Max) 0.3 kg to 30 kg\": type_level maximum_capacity {min: 0.3, max: 30} kg. Good.\n\nverification_scale_interval: {min: 0.05, max: null} g.\n\nminimum_capacity: \"20 × e\" → value: \"20 × e\". Hmm, maybe write as \"20 × e\" with unit null. Or \"20e\". I'll use \"20 × e\".\n\nmaximum_tare_effect: \"-Max\".\n\noperating_temperature_range: {min: -10, max: 40} °C.\n\nNow let me also consider \"Weighing ranges: Single-interval, multi-interval or multi-range\" → type_level \"weighing_range_types\": {value: \"single-interval, multi-interval or multi-range\"}.\n\nunit_id: leave null everywhere per rule 3 (only when certain; I'm not certain of exact unitsdb IDs, so null).\n\nLet me assemble JSON:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R76/2006-A-DK2-2023.07\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"page_total\": 3,\n    \"member_state\": \"DK\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2023-10-24\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"FORCE Certification A/S\",\n    \"person_responsible\": \"Jens Hovgård Jensen\",\n    \"person_title\": \"Certification Manager\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"ADW-E\", \"ADW-C\", \"AXM\", \"AGS\", \"VGS\"]\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [\n      {\"model_id\": \"ADW-E\", \"attributes\": {\"software_identification\": [\"CE031\", \"CE032\"]}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"ADW-C\", \"attributes\": {\"software_identification\": [\"CE033\"]}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"AXM\", \"attributes\": {\"software_identification\": [\"CE033\"]}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"AGS\", \"attributes\": {\"software_identification\": [\"CE034\"]}},\n      {\"model_id\": \"VGS\", \"attributes\": {\"software_identification\": [\"CE034\"]}}\n    ]\n  },\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": \"III, IIII\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []},\n      \"weighing_range_types\": {\"value\": \"single-interval, multi-interval or multi-range\", ...},\n      \"maximum_capacity\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0.3, \"max\": 30}, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg\"},\n      \"minimum_capacity\": {\"value\": \"20 × e\"},\n      \"verification_scale_interval\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0.05, \"max\": null}, \"unit_symbol\": \"g\"},\n      \"maximum_tare_effect\": {\"value\": \"-Max\"},\n      \"operating_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -10, \"max\": 40}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n      \"initial_zero_setting_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 20}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n      \"semi_automatic_zero_setting_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, max: 4}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n      \"zero_tracking_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 4}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n      \"permitted_weighing_units\": {\"value\": \"g, kg\"},\n      \"interface\": {\"value\": \"RS232\"},\n      \"interface_securing\": {\"value\": \"not required\"},\n      \"permitted_devices\": {\"value\": \"...\"} \n    },\n    \"model_level\": [],\n    \"config_level\": [\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"max_verification_scale_intervals\",\n        \"axis\": \"weighing range configuration\",\n        \"values\": [\n          {\"condition\": \"single-interval\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 6000}, \"unit_symbol\": null},\n          {\"condition\": \"multi-interval or multi-range\", \"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 10000}, \"unit_symbol\": null}\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"attribute\": \"power_supply_voltage\",\n        \"axis\": \"power source\",\n        \"values\": [\n          {\"condition\": \"external DC power supply\", \"value\": 12, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n          {\"condition\": \"rechargeable battery\", \"value\": 6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"}\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R76\",\n    \"edition\": 2006,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"accuracy_classes\": [\"III\", \"IIII\"]\n  },\n  \"test_reports\": [\n    {\"id\": \"122-29322.10\", \"date\": \"2023-03-30\", \"pages\": 119, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n    {\"id\": \"122-29322.90.20\", \"date\": \"2023-09-04\", \"pages\": 27, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n    {\"id\": \"122-29322\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}\n  ],\n  \"revision_history\": [\n    {\"revision\": \"Initial version\", \"date\": \"2023-10-24\"}\n  ],\n  \"footnotes\": []\n}\n\nConsiderations:\n- footnote_markers: include empty arrays or omit? The strict shape says every value MUST be object with value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers. Rule 2's example includes footnote_markers. I'll include unit_id: null and footnote_markers: [] consistently.\n- \"accuracy_class\" value \"III, IIII\" — maybe \"III and IIII\"? I'll use \"III, IIII\".\n- n max for multi-interval: \"≤2×5000\". I decided 10000. Let me reconsider once more... The rule: formulas/symbols keep as string. {min,max} bounds presumably numbers. \"2×5000\" as string in max field would violate number typing. I'll go with 10000. Hmm, actually — could I represent it as value: \"≤2×5000\"? Ranges rule says use {min,max}. I'll go 10000. Wait, but is it truly 10000? \"≤2×5000 (multi-interval/multi-range)\" — yes means n ≤ 2 × 5000 = 10000 total. Confident.\n\nActually, hmm, alternatively I could name attribute \"max_verification_scale_intervals\" and value for multi condition: {\"min\": null, \"max\": 10000}. Fine.\n\n- maximum_tare_effect: \"−Max\" — unicode minus. Use \"-Max\". Good.\n- permitted_devices string — I'll include a concise list. Actually, let me weigh: the section lists permitted devices with constraints. It's descriptive. I'll include it as one characteristic to preserve info:\n\n\"permitted_devices\": {\"value\": \"initial zero setting (≤20% Max); semi-automatic zero setting (≤4% Max); zero tracking (≤4% Max); semi-automatic and automatic subtractive tare devices; printing, totalization and unit toggling devices (g and kg only); sealed gravity compensation device; stable equilibrium, zero and net indicators\"}\n\nBut then the ≤20%/≤4% entries are duplicated with my numeric characteristics. That's fine — duplication preserves both structured and verbatim. Hmm, might be redundant. I'll keep the numeric zero-setting characteristics AND include permitted devices string but without the zero-setting percentages to reduce duplication? No — keep verbatim full list; duplication is acceptable. Actually, to keep output cleaner, maybe I'll keep the numeric ones and drop the big string, but include tare devices and indicators:\n\n- tare_device: {value: \"semi-automatic and automatic subtractive\"}\n- printing_device / totalization_device / unit_weight_toggling: {value: \"permitted (g and kg only)\"}? This is getting interpretive.\n\nDecision: include \"permitted_devices\" as a single verbatim-ish string. It preserves everything without interpretation. And keep the three numeric zero-setting ranges. Slight duplication OK.\n\nHmm wait, \"zero tracking (≤4% Max)\" — the ≤4% refers to the range around zero as fraction of Max. Fine.\n\n- RS232: \"the interface does not have to be secured\". I'll record:\n\"interface\": {\"value\": \"RS232\"},\n\"interface_securing_required\": {\"value\": false}\n\nIs boolean allowed? Schema: number | {min,max} | string. Boolean not listed. Use string \"not required\". I'll do \"interface_securing\": {\"value\": \"not required\"}.\n\n- certificate.scheme: \"A\" — from R76/2006-A-DK2. Yes.\n- recommendation.amendment: omit (not present).\n- page_total: 3 — from \"page 2 of 3\". Include.\n\n- \"Documentation file no. 122-29322\" → test_reports role documentation_file. Good.\n\n- member_state: \"DK\". Keep.\n\nCategory: Should I say \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" or \"Indicator\"? A&D ADW/AXM/AGS/VGS — these are weighing indicators/modules (ADW = A&D Weighing?). Under R76, certificates can cover indicators as separate instruments. Given \"Weighing ranges... Maximum capacity 0.3 kg to 30 kg\" — indicators themselves don't have Max in kg; that suggests complete instruments or weighing modules. Given uncertainty, \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" is the safest given R76. I'll use that.\n\nDates format: ISO. date_issued \"2023-10-24\". Report dates \"2023-03-30\", \"2023-09-04\".\n\nRevision history: revision \"Initial version\" — schema example says revision string e.g. \"0\". \"Initial version\" is the string present. Use it. changes: description is \"–\" → omit.\n\nLet me double check email/website/phone/address: not present. Omit.\n\noiml_issuer_id: not present. Omit.\n\nFinal JSON structure. Also ensure model_level: should I include accuracy class per model? No, classes apply to all.\n\nOne more: \"Max verification scale intervals (n) ≤6000 (single interval); ≤2×5000 (multi-interval/multi-range)\". Condition strings: \"single-interval\" and \"multi-interval or multi-range\". Good.\n\nWrite the JSON now, compact but readable. No markdown fences.",
